## Requirements

- Python 3.13 or newer
- Git recommended
- Linux or Windows

## Quick Setup — Linux

Clone or copy the repository and enter it:

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
cd axel-ai-lab
```

Run:

```bash
./scripts/setup_linux.sh
```

Activate the virtual environment:

```bash
source .venv/bin/activate
```

Run the Workbench:

```bash
axel-ai-lab --verbose
```

Alternatively:

```bash
python main.py --verbose
```

## Quick Setup — Windows PowerShell

Clone or copy the repository and enter it:

```powershell
git clone <repository-url>
cd axel-ai-lab
```

Run:

```powershell
.\scripts\setup_windows.ps1
```

Activate the environment:

```powershell
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

Run:

```powershell
axel-ai-lab --verbose
```

Alternatively:

```powershell
python main.py --verbose
```

If PowerShell prevents local scripts from running, the current session can temporarily allow them with:

```powershell
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process Bypass
```

## Manual Setup

If the setup scripts are not used, create a virtual environment manually.

Linux:

```bash
python3 -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate
```

Windows:

```powershell
py -3.13 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

Upgrade pip:

```bash
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
```

Install the complete development environment:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,tools]"
```

Create the local environment file.

Linux:

```bash
cp .env.example .env
```

Windows:

```powershell
Copy-Item .env.example .env
```

Then run:

```bash
axel-ai-lab --verbose
```

## Installation Options

Runtime dependencies only:

```bash
python -m pip install -e .
```

Runtime plus development tools:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

Complete repository environment:

```bash
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,tools]"
```

The complete environment includes pytest, mypy, and packages used by utilities inside `tools/`.

## Running Experiments

Default execution:

```bash
axel-ai-lab
```

Verbose execution:

```bash
axel-ai-lab --verbose
```

Help:

```bash
axel-ai-lab --help
```

Custom configuration:

```bash
axel-ai-lab --config configs/experiments.yaml
```

Custom output:

```bash
axel-ai-lab --output experiments/custom_results.json
```

## Experiment Configuration

The default configuration is:

```text
configs/experiments.yaml
```

Example:

```yaml
experiments:
  - name: small_benchmark
    sample_count: 10000
    iterations: 5
    enabled: true

  - name: medium_benchmark
    sample_count: 50000
    iterations: 10
    enabled: true

  - name: disabled_benchmark
    sample_count: 100000
    iterations: 20
    enabled: false
```

Invalid or disabled experiments are skipped before execution.

## Environment Variables

Local variables can be stored in:

```text
.env
```

Example:

```text
AXEL_AI_LAB_MODE=development
AXEL_AI_LAB_API_KEY=
```

The `.env` file is intentionally excluded from Git.

The repository contains:

```text
.env.example
```

as a safe template.

Never commit real API keys or credentials.

## Results

The latest completed experiment set is written to:

```text
experiments/results.json
```

Historical experiment records are appended to:

```text
experiments/history.csv
```

Each execution also receives a unique run directory:

```text
experiments/runs/<run_id>/
```

containing:

```text
result.json
```

A run ID looks similar to:

```text
run_20260915_101530_a3f72c
```

Future ML phases can store additional artifacts in the same directory, such as:

```text
model.pt
model.zip
config.json
evaluation.json
training_curve.png
confusion_matrix.png
```

## Testing

Run all tests:

```bash
pytest -v
```

Run a specific file:

```bash
pytest tests/test_storage.py -v
```

Stop on the first failure:

```bash
pytest -x
```

## Static Type Checking

Run:

```bash
mypy
```

The configured type-checking scope includes:

```text
main.py
src/
tests/
```

## Complete Verification

Run:

```bash
python scripts/verify.py
```

This executes:

```text
pytest
↓
mypy
↓
real Workbench execution
```

The verification is successful only when all three stages complete successfully.

## Development Workflow

A typical development cycle is:

```text
activate virtual environment
↓
edit code
↓
pytest
↓
mypy
↓
run Workbench
↓
git diff
↓
git add
↓
git commit
```

Useful Git commands:

```bash
git status
git diff
git log --oneline
```

